% -e over a rule that reads its own predicate twice.  Its two delta
% variants read p through different views: the one whose delta literal is
% the second p reads the first through the old view, the tuples older than
% its delta, marked `old`; the one whose delta literal is the first reads the
% second through the current view, everything up to its delta's end, which
% is left unmarked.  The rule runs only as its variants, so it shows no plan
% of its own.

e(a, b).
e(b, c).
e(c, d).

p(X, Y) :- e(X, Y).
p(X, Z) :- p(X, Y), p(Y, Z).

p(a, X)?
